  "body": "Two commits. The first expands the cycle from 12 days to 16. The second fixes the\nreason that was dangerous to do.\n\n## Why the second commit exists\n\nThe cycle was addressed positionally in three places that could disagree: a bundled\n`DAY_CONFIG`, a bundled `DAY_DESIGN`, and loose `workout-day-definition` documents.\nMoving a day silently repointed every exercise and every logged workout at whichever\nday took its slot. Applying a change meant clicking an admin button that deleted all\nthe data and reinserted it from `seed-data.js`, with no record of what ran or whether\nit finished.\n\nSo a 12→16 renumber wasn't a data change, it was an unversioned rewrite of live\nproduction data with no way to tell afterwards what had happened.\n\n## What changed\n\n**Days have a permanent slug.** The number is only a day's position in one version of\nthe model. Reordering the cycle changes numbers and nothing else.\n\n**The cycle is data.** A `workout-model` document per generation, exactly one active.\nSynergy 12 is retired rather than deleted, so a workout logged under Torso in 2025\nstill resolves to Torso.\n\n**Logs are faithful records.** Each stores `daySlug`, `dayNumber`, `dayName` and\n`modelVersion` as they were on the day it happened. Nothing later edits them.\n\n**Migrations replace the seed step.** Numbered, forward-only, shipped in the same image\nas the code that expects them, run at pod startup before any route is mounted. A\nfailure exits the process and fails the rollout — a half-migrated database serving\ntraffic is worse than a deploy that stops.\n\nCosmos has no cross-document transaction, so a migration can die partway and be retried\nfrom the top. Each one is written to survive that, and `npm run migrate:dry-run` proves\nit by replaying against the already-migrated result.\n\nMigrations auto-apply only in the cluster (`RUN_MIGRATIONS_ON_BOOT`). Local development\npoints at the live database, so `npm run dev` reports what's pending instead of applying\nit — otherwise starting a dev server on a branch would push a half-written migration to\nproduction.\n\n## Verification\n\n`npm run migrate:dry-run` reads production read-only, runs both migrations in memory,\nprints the resulting cycle, and replays them to check they're safe to retry.\n\nIt caught a real bug before it shipped: Dumbbell Bench Press legitimately sits on two\ndays (Pecs Mobility at 20lb, and Push), and the first version of 002 collapsed them into\none entry and then tried to delete the same document twice.\n\nThe app was then built against a snapshot generated from the post-migration data and\nloaded as an anonymous visitor: 16-segment dial, D11 Pecs (Mobility) as today, 52\nexercises across 16 day filters, Cycle tab rendering entirely from the model. History\nkeeps its own past — Aug 21 still reads D07 Torso in Torso's color, July 26 still reads\nD03 Hamstrings, under a cycle that has neither day.\n\n## Notes\n\n`frontend/public/snapshot.db` is regenerated in this PR deliberately. It's baked into\nthe image; left on the old schema, anonymous visitors would read a snapshot the new\nfrontend can't parse until the 4-hourly cron caught up.\n\nOn merge, the deploy applies both migrations and the current-day pointer lands on\n`pecs-mobility` — the same workout that was already coming up next, so the cutover\ndoesn't change what tomorrow is.\n\nTwo eslint errors in `App.jsx` (`__BUILD_NUMBER__`, `isAdmin`) are pre-existing and\nuntouched.\n\nContinuous backup for the shared Cosmos account is being handled separately in\ninfra-bootstrap; it's an account-level, one-way change that affects the other apps on\nthat account, so it doesn't belong here.\n",
